item.description:
arXiv:2610.26705v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: Let $P$ be a trigonometric polynomial with only real zeros. We prove that at most $\frac{2}{3}$ of its zeros coincide with the zeros of the Riemann zeta function $\zeta(s)$ on the critical line. We also show that \begin{equation*}
  \sup_{\substack{T\leq x\leq2T\\ P(x)=0}}
  \log \left|\zeta\left(\frac{1}{2}+ix\right)\right|
  \geq
  (1+o(1))\sqrt{\frac{\log T}{6\log\log T}}. \end{equation*} These results extend the corresponding theorems of Li and Radziwill on the zeros of $\zeta(s)$ along vertical arithmetic progressions. The main novelty in our approach is the use of the work of Kurasov and Sarnak and Lee-Yang representations of Alon-Cohen-Vinzant, which show that the corresponding measure $\sum_{P(\gamma)=0} {\bf \delta}_\gamma$ is crystalline.
